{
  "id": "Compound::DB06694 - Gene::151",
  "verbalization": "Xylometazoline, a nasal decongestant, is shown to bind to Adra2c, a variant of the alpha-2 adrenergic receptor, which is involved in the regulation of adrenergic signaling. This binding is further linked to the participation of Adra2c in the adrenaline signaling pathway through the alpha-2 adrenergic receptor, which in turn is associated with Adra2b. The evidence suggests that Xylometazoline exerts its effects by modulating the activity of Adra2b through the activation of the alpha-2 adrenergic receptor pathway. The consistent binding and participation relationships across the three paths indicate a mechanistic link between Xylometazoline and Adra2b, particularly through the involvement of Adra2c in the signaling cascade. The observed scores suggest a strong and reliable interaction, supported by multiple independent pathways, which supports the hypothesis that Xylometazoline influences Adra2b via the alpha-2 adrenergic receptor mechanism.",
